Author: Ryan Gibb <ryan@freumh.org>
Date:   Fri Aug 21 14:51:36 2026 +0100

    Don't fabricate an end date when none was given
    
    `add` defaulted the end date to the start date whenever a start date was
    present, so `--time 14:00` without `--end-time` built a DATE-typed end
    against a DATE-TIME start and was rejected outright:
    
        $ caled add "Meeting" --date today --time 14:00
        Error: If the start is a datetime the end must also be a datetime.
    
    RFC 5545 3.6.1 already defines what a VEVENT with no DTEND means -- one
    day for a DATE start, and the same instant as DTSTART for a DATE-TIME
    start -- so there is nothing to guess. Drop the defaulting and leave the
    end unset; keep inferring the end date only when an end *time* was given,
    which is what it is actually for.
    
    An all-day `--date X` now writes DTSTART;VALUE=DATE with no DTEND rather
    than an explicit DTEND of X+1. Both mean a one-day event under the RFC.
    
    Two manpage examples used --time with no --end-time and so could not run
    as written. Both now pass an end time, which is what you almost always
    want: without one the event is instantaneous, not open-ended.
    
